1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Subtitles added by Hanson Hu

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:55,000
Episode 21 - A Big Fish in a Little Pond

3
00:01:38,920 --> 00:01:40,780
I really like this one, Susan. Do you?

4
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:42,880
Turn around, Michelle. Let me see the back of it.

5
00:01:43,680 --> 00:01:46,160
I like it a lot. It fits well.

6
00:01:46,160 --> 00:01:49,640
It doesn't need any alterations. We must've bought the right size.

7
00:01:49,760 --> 00:01:51,840
I like the color. She looks good in blue.

8
00:01:51,840 --> 00:01:53,340
I like blue, too, Daddy.

9
00:01:53,680 --> 00:01:55,960
Try on the skirt and blouse outfit, Michelle.

10
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:57,440
The one that Daddy wanted you to wear.

11
00:01:57,440 --> 00:01:58,040
OK.

12
00:02:00,640 --> 00:02:03,380
Michelle has been a different kid since we've been married.

13
00:02:03,380 --> 00:02:04,760
She's never been happier.

14
00:02:05,360 --> 00:02:07,440
And I've never been happier, Harry.

15
00:02:08,600 --> 00:02:11,740
I love her very much. She's been a joy.

16
00:02:13,020 --> 00:02:14,800
We're very lucky, the three of us...

17
00:02:15,520 --> 00:02:18,160
and becoming part of the Stewart family, too.

18
00:02:18,880 --> 00:02:20,720
We couldn't ask for anything more, could we?

19
00:02:21,660 --> 00:02:22,440
Well...

20
00:02:23,420 --> 00:02:27,400
Well...? Could we ask for anything more?

21
00:02:29,260 --> 00:02:31,120
Well... we...

22
00:02:31,320 --> 00:02:32,540
How do you like it, Daddy?

23
00:02:32,540 --> 00:02:34,120
It's my favorite outfit.

24
00:02:34,200 --> 00:02:38,080
It's good for every day. It will be good for school, Michelle.

25
00:02:38,120 --> 00:02:41,120
I like it, too. I always like skirts that go like this.

26
00:02:42,440 --> 00:02:45,620
Do you want to see the winter jacket on me, Susan?

27
00:02:45,700 --> 00:02:48,500
Yes, I do. Change back into your jeans,

28
00:02:48,500 --> 00:02:50,880
and put on the new winter jacket we bought today.

29
00:02:50,880 --> 00:02:51,720
OK.

30
00:02:58,580 --> 00:03:01,360
What did you mean by "well"?

31
00:03:02,280 --> 00:03:03,760
You had something on your mind

32
00:03:03,760 --> 00:03:06,840
when I said we couldn't ask for anything more.

33
00:03:08,560 --> 00:03:09,980
Is everything all right?

34
00:03:10,720 --> 00:03:12,380
Everything is fine, Michelle.

35
00:03:12,880 --> 00:03:16,320
Let's take a look at the winter jacket. Come on over here, honey.

36
00:03:16,660 --> 00:03:19,120
It's kind of warm.

37
00:03:19,120 --> 00:03:22,080
It is. That's why we bought it for you.

38
00:03:22,200 --> 00:03:24,840
This will be a perfect jacket for the wintertime

39
00:03:25,580 --> 00:03:26,840
when it's very cold out.

40
00:03:27,980 --> 00:03:31,840
But it's kind of small also. We must've bought the wrong size.

41
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:33,700
Looks like we should've bought a bigger one.

42
00:03:33,880 --> 00:03:35,680
I guess we'll have to exchange it, too.

43
00:03:35,680 --> 00:03:36,940
I'm sure the store has others.

44
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:39,840
I look silly! It is too small!

45
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:42,240
You're growing so fast, Michelle.

46
00:03:42,440 --> 00:03:44,720
Can I take it off? I'm hot!

47
00:03:44,820 --> 00:03:48,160
Sure. Put it back in your room, and we'll hang everything up later.

48
00:03:53,800 --> 00:03:56,700
Well, are you going to tell me what's on your mind, Harry?

49
00:03:59,040 --> 00:04:02,860
I have been offered a job with a major accounting company

50
00:04:02,860 --> 00:04:03,880
in Los Angeles.

51
00:04:08,080 --> 00:04:11,500
I have been offered a job with a major accounting company

52
00:04:11,500 --> 00:04:12,560
in Los Angeles.

53
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:17,440
Los Angeles? That's a big decision.

54
00:04:17,480 --> 00:04:24,440
I know. It will also affect you and your job, if we decide to go.

55
00:04:24,880 --> 00:04:30,880
Wow! It sure will. But first tell me about the job, Harry.

56
00:04:31,160 --> 00:04:34,540
If it's a good one, then we'll make it work for us.

57
00:04:37,120 --> 00:04:40,280
I have a client in the garment business, on Seventh Avenue.

58
00:04:40,320 --> 00:04:42,380
I do his taxes every year.

59
00:04:42,780 --> 00:04:45,880
He has a big sales office in Los Angeles,

60
00:04:45,880 --> 00:04:47,340
and the company in Los Angeles

61
00:04:47,340 --> 00:04:49,040
that does his major accounting work

62
00:04:49,220 --> 00:04:52,800
is looking for an executive. And he recommended me.

63
00:04:53,440 --> 00:04:54,920
That's wonderful, Harry.

64
00:04:56,080 --> 00:04:58,380
Yes, but it would mean that we'd have to move to L.A.

65
00:05:00,400 --> 00:05:01,860
What about the salary?

66
00:05:03,200 --> 00:05:04,920
The real discussion comes tomorrow.

67
00:05:07,480 --> 00:05:12,600
Susan, I don't plan to make any decisions

68
00:05:12,640 --> 00:05:15,280
until I have a chance to talk with you about it.

69
00:05:16,520 --> 00:05:17,940
I understand, Harry.

70
00:05:18,380 --> 00:05:20,540
And I don't have to make a quick decision.

71
00:05:20,540 --> 00:05:23,000
They know that I'm married and that I have a family.

72
00:05:24,040 --> 00:05:26,400
Well, there's a lot to think about.

73
00:05:27,580 --> 00:05:29,540
If it's a good job,

74
00:05:29,540 --> 00:05:31,880
then I've got to do some thinking about

75
00:05:32,060 --> 00:05:34,280
my career opportunities in Los Angeles.

76
00:05:36,240 --> 00:05:37,760
Is everything OK?

77
00:05:39,160 --> 00:05:39,920
Yes, honey.

78
00:05:56,900 --> 00:06:02,080
Harry told Susan he was thinking about a new job in California.

79
00:06:02,400 --> 00:06:04,080
She must have been surprised.

80
00:06:04,760 --> 00:06:07,160
You can see it in her face.

81
00:06:07,400 --> 00:06:08,720
It's clear to me.

82
00:06:08,840 --> 00:06:10,480
It's easy to see.

83
00:06:10,520 --> 00:06:12,200
She must have been surprised.

84
00:06:12,780 --> 00:06:13,560
Must have been?

85
00:06:13,640 --> 00:06:17,440
You mean "must have been."

86
00:06:17,440 --> 00:06:19,780
We can say "must've" in conversation,

87
00:06:20,400 --> 00:06:23,040
and sometimes we don't pronounce the "v,"

88
00:06:23,040 --> 00:06:24,720
and so you hear "musta."

89
00:06:24,720 --> 00:06:27,240
She must have been surprised.

90
00:06:27,240 --> 00:06:28,520
Yes, yes, yes.

91
00:06:28,520 --> 00:06:30,860
I can see that she was surprised.

92
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:32,120
It's very obvious.

93
00:06:32,520 --> 00:06:33,460
What, "musta"?

94
00:06:33,480 --> 00:06:35,420
Well, you don't have to say it,

95
00:06:35,420 --> 00:06:38,240
but sometimes you're gonna hear it.

96
00:06:38,240 --> 00:06:40,380
We must have bought the wrong size.

97
00:06:42,400 --> 00:06:44,040
We must have bought the wrong size.

98
00:06:44,420 --> 00:06:47,720
They must have bought the wrong size.

99
00:06:47,720 --> 00:06:51,240
You can see that the jacket is much too small.

100
00:06:51,240 --> 00:06:53,600
It's clear to me.

101
00:06:53,640 --> 00:06:55,200
It's easy to see.

102
00:06:55,200 --> 00:06:57,720
They must have bought the wrong size.

103
00:06:57,800 --> 00:06:59,100
No kidding.

104
00:06:59,160 --> 00:07:00,640
Is everything OK?

105
00:07:01,100 --> 00:07:02,800
She looks worried.

106
00:07:02,800 --> 00:07:04,920
Yes, I can see that.

107
00:07:04,980 --> 00:07:07,880
Do you think she heard Susan and Harry talking

108
00:07:07,880 --> 00:07:10,120
about moving to Los Angeles?

109
00:07:10,120 --> 00:07:13,320
Well, evidently she heard something,

110
00:07:14,120 --> 00:07:17,220
or she saw the serious look on their faces.

111
00:07:17,440 --> 00:07:20,280
Michelle is obviously concerned.

112
00:07:20,340 --> 00:07:22,100
She saw something.

113
00:07:22,660 --> 00:07:25,020
Yes, she must have seen something.

114
00:07:25,020 --> 00:07:27,120
She musta seen something.

115
00:07:27,620 --> 00:07:30,260
Or she heard something.

116
00:07:30,260 --> 00:07:33,280
Yes, she must have heard something.

117
00:07:33,280 --> 00:07:35,060
She musta heard something.

118
00:07:36,680 --> 00:07:38,260
It's clear to me.

119
00:07:38,840 --> 00:07:40,120
It's easy to see.

120
00:07:40,240 --> 00:07:45,480
I must have been crazy to come here.

121
00:08:42,760 --> 00:08:47,760
Hello. Hello there.

122
00:08:50,480 --> 00:08:52,400
Are you ready for lunch with your grandpa?

123
00:08:52,400 --> 00:08:57,200
Oh! Hi, Grandpa. Yes, of course, I am, but my mind isn't.

124
00:08:57,200 --> 00:08:58,440
What's the matter, Susan?

125
00:08:58,760 --> 00:09:00,500
A real dilemma.

126
00:09:00,640 --> 00:09:02,520
Does it have anything to do with you and Harry?

127
00:09:03,560 --> 00:09:06,480
Yes, but I don't know where to start.

128
00:09:07,640 --> 00:09:12,240
Maybe I can help. Tell me what it is, Susan.

129
00:09:14,240 --> 00:09:16,920
Thanks, Grandpa. Please sit down.

130
00:09:20,720 --> 00:09:23,200
Oh! Harry has been offered a job in Los Angeles.

131
00:09:23,200 --> 00:09:27,160
Well, this is something to think about.

132
00:09:27,160 --> 00:09:31,520
There are so many things to consider. There's Michelle.

133
00:09:31,520 --> 00:09:36,340
I wonder if a move would be a bad thing for her. And my job.

134
00:09:36,340 --> 00:09:39,300
I don't know if I can get a good job in Los Angeles.

135
00:09:39,360 --> 00:09:41,880
And what about our family?

136
00:09:44,200 --> 00:09:45,560
Can I tell you what I think?

137
00:09:45,560 --> 00:09:47,280
Tell me.

138
00:09:47,300 --> 00:09:51,200
I think that you're very successful

139
00:09:51,480 --> 00:09:55,560
and that you have a fantastic reputation in the toy industry.

140
00:09:55,680 --> 00:09:58,880
I think you could talk to Mr. Marchetta,

141
00:10:00,080 --> 00:10:00,800
and I think

142
00:10:00,800 --> 00:10:05,000
he could help you find a real good job in Los Angeles.

143
00:10:05,320 --> 00:10:07,040
He was very helpful to me, remember?

144
00:10:08,200 --> 00:10:10,320
I suppose I could call him.

145
00:10:10,640 --> 00:10:14,080
But I'm not so sure that I want to leave New York,

146
00:10:14,080 --> 00:10:15,840
you, and the rest of our family.

147
00:10:16,200 --> 00:10:17,600
Well, I'm not going to kid you, Susan.

148
00:10:17,600 --> 00:10:19,080
You know we'd all miss you.

149
00:10:20,280 --> 00:10:23,480
But this should be your decision.

150
00:10:23,720 --> 00:10:27,380
It's something that only you and Harry can work out.

151
00:10:29,740 --> 00:10:32,520
If moving to L.A. is in Harry's best interest,

152
00:10:33,120 --> 00:10:35,980
I have to do what I can do to support him.

153
00:10:36,880 --> 00:10:38,440
In every marriage,

154
00:10:38,720 --> 00:10:42,360
sacrifices have to be made by one partner from time to time.

155
00:10:42,500 --> 00:10:45,300
And what about Michelle?

156
00:10:46,520 --> 00:10:48,100
Well, what do you think?

157
00:10:48,760 --> 00:10:51,680
I think Michelle is better off staying where she is.

158
00:10:51,680 --> 00:10:53,520
What does she think?

159
00:10:54,440 --> 00:10:55,740
I don't know for sure.

160
00:10:56,380 --> 00:10:57,880
Well, you'll have to ask her.

161
00:10:58,120 --> 00:11:00,940
I think I'm going to have a talk with Mr. Marchetta

162
00:11:00,940 --> 00:11:03,040
and get his feelings about my leaving.

163
00:11:03,220 --> 00:11:05,840
And about helping me find a job in Los Angeles.

164
00:11:06,800 --> 00:11:08,100
Good idea.

165
00:11:08,240 --> 00:11:10,560
I'll call him right now. No point in delaying.

166
00:11:10,560 --> 00:11:12,080
Thanks, Grandpa.

167
00:11:22,680 --> 00:11:25,940
Harry, it's the perfect job for you. You'll love it.

168
00:11:27,080 --> 00:11:29,020
It's a big decision for me, Bill.

169
00:11:29,200 --> 00:11:31,220
And I have to discuss it with my wife.

170
00:11:31,240 --> 00:11:32,960
I don't know if it's right for her.

171
00:11:32,960 --> 00:11:36,400
She'll love it. It's a once-in-a-lifetime offer, Harry.

172
00:11:36,880 --> 00:11:38,620
OK. Tell it to me again.

173
00:11:38,620 --> 00:11:39,940
The company is Craft and Craft,

174
00:11:41,120 --> 00:11:43,240
the biggest accounting company in the country.

175
00:11:43,240 --> 00:11:45,460
I know the company well. It's big.

176
00:11:45,460 --> 00:11:46,280
The biggest.

177
00:11:46,660 --> 00:11:51,000
Yeah, yeah. The biggest. When do I have to let you know?

178
00:11:51,980 --> 00:11:55,080
Talk it over. Think it over. Let me know by the end of the week.

179
00:11:56,000 --> 00:11:57,080
When would we have to move?

180
00:11:57,080 --> 00:11:58,560
As soon as possible.

181
00:11:59,860 --> 00:12:02,380
I also have my daughter to consider.

182
00:12:02,380 --> 00:12:04,720
I don't want to interrupt her school year.

183
00:12:05,880 --> 00:12:07,420
Let me know by the end of the week.

184
00:12:07,600 --> 00:12:09,480
It's a great opportunity for you, Harry.

185
00:12:09,480 --> 00:12:10,240
Believe me.

186
00:12:10,340 --> 00:12:11,080
I know.

187
00:12:11,360 --> 00:12:13,480
Craft and Craft is the biggest in the country.

188
00:12:13,720 --> 00:12:16,480
Yeah, I know. The biggest.

189
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:32,280
I think Michelle is asleep now. Let's talk.

190
00:12:39,480 --> 00:12:40,920
I met with Bill York today.

191
00:12:40,920 --> 00:12:44,640
And I talked with Mr. Marchetta. Did York make the offer?

192
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:46,800
Yup. He asked me if I want the job.

193
00:12:46,860 --> 00:12:49,300
That's exciting, Harry. What was it?

194
00:12:49,520 --> 00:12:50,780
A vice-presidency

195
00:12:50,820 --> 00:12:52,500
with the biggest accounting company in the country -

196
00:12:52,920 --> 00:12:54,120
Craft and Craft.

197
00:12:54,740 --> 00:12:56,600
Aren't you excited about that?

198
00:12:56,600 --> 00:13:00,040
Sure I am. But there's so much more to consider.

199
00:13:01,320 --> 00:13:03,160
I talked to Mr. Marchetta.

200
00:13:03,400 --> 00:13:06,040
What did he say? Did you tell him about me?

201
00:13:06,320 --> 00:13:08,920
Of course, Harry. I want what's best for you,

202
00:13:08,920 --> 00:13:11,720
and I think I can get a good job

203
00:13:11,720 --> 00:13:12,680
through Mr. Marchetta

204
00:13:12,680 --> 00:13:13,800
in Los Angeles also.

205
00:13:13,800 --> 00:13:14,600
You're kidding.

206
00:13:14,600 --> 00:13:15,880
No, I'm not kidding.

207
00:13:15,880 --> 00:13:18,420
I talked with him, and he understands completely.

208
00:13:18,800 --> 00:13:21,400
He has a major toy buyer in Los Angeles,

209
00:13:21,400 --> 00:13:23,720
and he's pretty sure that I can get a good job there.

210
00:13:23,860 --> 00:13:25,100
Unbelievable!

211
00:13:25,100 --> 00:13:28,020
But I think we should talk to Michelle about all of this.

212
00:13:28,740 --> 00:13:31,200
You're right. We'll talk to her about it.

213
00:13:32,680 --> 00:13:35,880
And how do you feel about taking the job in Los Angeles?

214
00:13:36,360 --> 00:13:37,340
How should I feel?

215
00:13:37,340 --> 00:13:39,060
It's the biggest company in the country.

216
00:13:39,420 --> 00:13:41,160
Well, then you feel good about taking it?

217
00:13:42,080 --> 00:13:46,480
Well... I feel fine about it. Why shouldn't I?

218
00:14:00,620 --> 00:14:02,920
Harry, it's the perfect job for you.

219
00:14:02,920 --> 00:14:03,680
You'll love it.

220
00:14:05,420 --> 00:14:06,920
It's a big decision for me, Bill.

221
00:14:07,140 --> 00:14:09,320
And I have to discuss it with my wife.

222
00:14:10,080 --> 00:14:14,480
Is moving away to Los Angeles a good idea for Susan?

223
00:14:15,080 --> 00:14:16,360
Is it right for her?

224
00:14:29,580 --> 00:14:31,600
I don't know if it's right for her.

225
00:14:35,400 --> 00:14:37,280
What does Susan think?

226
00:14:37,280 --> 00:14:39,300
What is she asking herself?

227
00:14:39,300 --> 00:14:40,680
Her question is...

228
00:14:40,680 --> 00:14:43,100
Can I get a good job in Los Angeles?

229
00:14:54,840 --> 00:14:57,640
I don't know if I can get a good job in Los Angeles.

230
00:15:00,920 --> 00:15:03,240
And what about Michelle?

231
00:15:06,280 --> 00:15:08,560
Would Michelle like to move?

232
00:15:08,760 --> 00:15:11,800
Or would a move be a bad thing for her?

233
00:15:23,440 --> 00:15:26,520
I wonder if a move would be a bad thing for her.

234
00:15:29,640 --> 00:15:32,640
So, Harry, do you want the job?

235
00:15:32,920 --> 00:15:33,940
That's the question.

236
00:15:33,940 --> 00:15:36,460
Hmm, do I want the job?

237
00:15:48,780 --> 00:15:50,200
He asked me if I want the job.

238
00:15:53,040 --> 00:15:54,320
So what do you think?

239
00:15:54,320 --> 00:15:55,840
Will Harry take the job?

240
00:15:55,940 --> 00:15:58,500
I don't know if he'll take the job.

241
00:16:50,160 --> 00:16:53,960
I love my school. I have so many good friends there now.

242
00:16:53,960 --> 00:16:56,540
I wouldn't miss a day even if I were really sick.

243
00:16:57,320 --> 00:17:00,640
Come and sit down for a minute, Michelle.

244
00:17:02,800 --> 00:17:05,580
I'd like to talk to you about something.

245
00:17:05,840 --> 00:17:07,180
Something important.

246
00:17:07,520 --> 00:17:09,480
What's wrong, Susan?

247
00:17:09,640 --> 00:17:11,280
Oh, there's nothing wrong, Michelle.

248
00:17:11,500 --> 00:17:14,320
But your daddy and I are talking about something

249
00:17:14,320 --> 00:17:15,680
that I'd like your opinion about.

250
00:17:15,880 --> 00:17:18,800
I know. I heard you talking about it

251
00:17:19,040 --> 00:17:21,320
the other night when I was trying on my new clothes.

252
00:17:21,920 --> 00:17:23,780
It's about moving to Los Angeles.

253
00:17:25,220 --> 00:17:28,160
You're right. How do you feel about it?

254
00:17:29,480 --> 00:17:34,140
Well, I really wouldn't want to move, but...

255
00:17:34,200 --> 00:17:34,600
But?

256
00:17:36,680 --> 00:17:38,600
But if you and Daddy wanted to,

257
00:17:38,780 --> 00:17:41,760
I guess you know what's best for the family and for me.

258
00:17:42,740 --> 00:17:44,880
That's very considerate of you, Michelle.

259
00:17:45,120 --> 00:17:46,840
But what about your friends?

260
00:17:47,080 --> 00:17:48,280
I'd miss them a lot,

261
00:17:48,440 --> 00:17:51,280
but I know what it feels like to miss someone.

262
00:17:52,380 --> 00:17:55,160
Honey, we don't have to move

263
00:17:55,160 --> 00:17:56,860
if you're not going to be happy about it.

264
00:17:57,080 --> 00:17:58,560
Does Daddy want to move?

265
00:17:59,060 --> 00:18:03,100
I think so. He's going to tell us tonight about the job offer.

266
00:18:10,480 --> 00:18:12,120
Well, how was everybody's day today?

267
00:18:12,720 --> 00:18:14,660
Michelle was chosen to do

268
00:18:14,660 --> 00:18:16,560
the school poster for the play this year.

269
00:18:16,560 --> 00:18:19,240
Congratulations, Michelle! That's something!

270
00:18:19,960 --> 00:18:21,560
And how was your day, Susan?

271
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:24,400
I see you're in a good mood.

272
00:18:24,400 --> 00:18:27,120
Why don't you tell us about your day.

273
00:18:27,560 --> 00:18:29,860
I met with Bill York.

274
00:18:30,480 --> 00:18:32,400
It's OK to talk about it, Harry.

275
00:18:32,400 --> 00:18:33,880
Michelle knows all about it.

276
00:18:34,460 --> 00:18:35,160
Really?

277
00:18:35,820 --> 00:18:37,060
Really, Harry.

278
00:18:37,720 --> 00:18:39,720
Michelle and I have all kinds of feelings

279
00:18:39,720 --> 00:18:42,400
about leaving New York, the family, and friends.

280
00:18:42,960 --> 00:18:46,500
But if you think you should take the job, we're behind you.

281
00:18:48,040 --> 00:18:49,360
What about Michelle's school?

282
00:18:49,420 --> 00:18:51,680
We'll move after the school term.

283
00:18:52,200 --> 00:18:53,480
What about her new friends?

284
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:58,120
I'll make new friends wherever we are as long as we're together.

285
00:19:00,040 --> 00:19:01,440
We're a family, Harry.

286
00:19:01,680 --> 00:19:04,680
Whatever you think is right for you is right for us.

287
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:10,680
I am so touched. The two of you are really something.

288
00:19:12,240 --> 00:19:13,080
We love you, Daddy.

289
00:19:13,320 --> 00:19:15,040
And I love you.

290
00:19:17,880 --> 00:19:20,440
OK. Now, tell us about your talk with Mr. York.

291
00:19:20,440 --> 00:19:21,600
Did you take the job?

292
00:19:21,600 --> 00:19:22,160
Nope.

293
00:19:22,640 --> 00:19:23,380
What?

294
00:19:23,940 --> 00:19:25,960
No? You didn't take it?

295
00:19:25,960 --> 00:19:28,560
No, I did not take the job.

296
00:19:29,080 --> 00:19:30,480
But, Daddy, I thought...

297
00:19:30,760 --> 00:19:33,000
Harry, you didn't turn it down because of me...

298
00:19:33,040 --> 00:19:33,820
Or me?

299
00:19:33,840 --> 00:19:36,180
No, no. I turned it down because of me.

300
00:19:36,600 --> 00:19:37,320
How's that?

301
00:19:39,040 --> 00:19:41,760
Well, I began to think about you and about Michelle,

302
00:19:41,760 --> 00:19:43,880
and then I asked myself,

303
00:19:43,880 --> 00:19:47,400
do I really want to work for the biggest company in the country?

304
00:19:47,900 --> 00:19:48,640
And?

305
00:19:49,200 --> 00:19:50,160
And I don't.

306
00:19:50,160 --> 00:19:52,120
I went into business for myself

307
00:19:52,120 --> 00:19:54,040
because I like being my own boss.

308
00:19:55,340 --> 00:19:58,720
I run my own company. I'm a big fish in a little pond.

309
00:19:59,280 --> 00:20:01,760
I'm not really sure I want to be a little fish in a big pond.

310
00:20:03,480 --> 00:20:05,760
Oh, Daddy, does that mean we don't have to move?

311
00:20:05,760 --> 00:20:06,760
That's right, sweetheart.

312
00:20:07,040 --> 00:20:08,720
Are you sure?

313
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:11,520
I couldn't be more sure, Susan.

314
00:20:13,360 --> 00:20:15,600
I'm glad if you are, Harry.

315
00:20:15,600 --> 00:20:16,400
And besides,

316
00:20:16,400 --> 00:20:18,640
how could I live in Los Angeles

317
00:20:18,660 --> 00:20:20,840
when all my favorite people live here?

318
00:20:21,600 --> 00:20:22,800
Your favorite people?

319
00:20:23,220 --> 00:20:24,140
Who's that?

320
00:20:24,960 --> 00:20:26,440
The Stewart family, of course.

321
00:20:45,640 --> 00:20:48,660
I kept asking myself, do I really want to work

322
00:20:48,660 --> 00:20:50,840
for the biggest company in the country?

323
00:20:50,880 --> 00:20:53,420
The company is Craft and Craft,

324
00:20:53,520 --> 00:20:56,440
the biggest accounting company in the country.

325
00:20:56,440 --> 00:20:57,520
I know the company well.

326
00:20:57,640 --> 00:20:58,140
It's big.

327
00:20:58,140 --> 00:20:59,880
The biggest.

328
00:21:00,080 --> 00:21:01,440
And they're big, all right.

329
00:21:01,640 --> 00:21:04,540
With offices all over the country.

330
00:21:04,740 --> 00:21:08,160
Altogether, they have 86 offices in the U.S. alone.

331
00:21:08,360 --> 00:21:12,640
And one of those offices is in Los Angeles, and that's where I'd be.

332
00:21:15,240 --> 00:21:16,600
Harry Bennett.

333
00:21:16,800 --> 00:21:20,400
Just one accountant in a huge company full of accountants.

334
00:21:20,600 --> 00:21:24,680
I'd be one little fish in a big pond full of accountants.

335
00:21:25,280 --> 00:21:26,400
Do you want...

336
00:21:26,400 --> 00:21:31,880
Do you want to be a little fish in a big pond?

337
00:21:32,080 --> 00:21:35,320
I'm not really sure I want to be a little fish in a big pond.

338
00:21:35,520 --> 00:21:37,620
I like being my own boss.

339
00:21:37,820 --> 00:21:39,720
His own boss.

340
00:21:39,920 --> 00:21:41,900
I work hard, but I make my own schedule.

341
00:21:41,900 --> 00:21:44,420
I can take time off when I want to.

342
00:21:44,620 --> 00:21:47,000
And I choose my own clients.

343
00:21:47,200 --> 00:21:48,280
Yeah.

344
00:21:48,280 --> 00:21:51,860
I have a client in the garment business, on Seventh Avenue.

345
00:21:52,060 --> 00:21:54,280
I do his taxes every year.

346
00:21:54,480 --> 00:21:56,620
He and my other clients know me personally.

347
00:21:56,620 --> 00:21:57,880
And I know them.

348
00:21:58,080 --> 00:22:00,020
He knows them all.

349
00:22:00,220 --> 00:22:03,120
They tell me I make a big difference in their businesses.

350
00:22:03,320 --> 00:22:05,000
And I do it my way.

351
00:22:05,200 --> 00:22:07,760
As president of my own company.

352
00:22:07,960 --> 00:22:09,700
Yeah.

353
00:22:10,440 --> 00:22:13,280
I guess you could say I like being a big fish

354
00:22:13,480 --> 00:22:15,120
in my own little pond.

355
00:22:15,320 --> 00:22:17,040
Yeah.

356
00:22:17,240 --> 00:22:22,760
He wants to be a big fish in a little pond.

357
00:22:22,960 --> 00:22:25,560
A little pond.

358
00:22:25,760 --> 00:22:27,600
He wants to be a big fish in a little pond.

